from bisect import bisect_right

def lis_length(nums: list[int]) -> int:
    if not nums:
        return 0

    # Initialize the smallest extreme value for the lis
    smallest = [-1]

    for num in nums:
        # Find the index of the smallest number in the smallest list that is greater than the current number
        index = bisect_right(smallest, num)
        # If the current number is greater than all in smallest, append it
        if index == len(smallest):
            smallest.append(num)
        # Otherwise, replace the smallest number at the found index
        else:
            smallest[index] = num

    # The length of the smallest list is the length of the longest increasing subsequence
    return len(smallest)
```

This solution uses binary search to keep track of the smallest elements in the increasing subsequence, updating the smallest list in O(n log n) time, which meets the requirement of O(n log n) time complexity. The space complexity is O(n) due to the storage of the smallest elements.

